  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Trevor McDonald couldn't hold the fourth inning and nothing after that mattered. The White Sox scored seven runs before anyone warmed up properly, and by the time the Giants found their bats in the fifth, the game was already decided. This is what early pitching collapses look like in a three-run division. One bad stretch erases whatever comes next.",
    "what_this_means": "McDonald lasted 3.2 innings and gave up seven runs on three hits, the kind of catastrophic outing that defines losses before the game gets interesting. The bullpen did its job afterward. Borucki, Miller, and Brubaker combined for 5.1 scoreless innings. But a lead that large doesn't get caught. The Giants scored on four of their eight RISP chances and had something to show for it, but it was theater in a game decided by inning four. Twelve strikeouts is not production. It's noise. They were chasing from the second inning forward and never closed the distance that mattered.",
    "what_to_watch": "Bryan Hudson holds a 1.57 ERA and takes the mound for Chicago tonight. Houser has worked his recent games sharply. 2.94 over his last four starts. And will need every bit of that form.",
